winewayland.drv: Send the button release of a press whose surface this process destroyed.

Seen in the lab (CapCut 9.7, 11-03/11-04): after Escape closed the menu, the next click on "Menu" did not reopen it.
This patch does NOT fix that symptom (see "Lab evidence"); it fixes a Windows delta found while tracing it: when
CapCut closes its menu because the user clicked outside it, the click's WM_LBUTTONUP never reaches any window and
Wine keeps the left button down until the next click.

Mechanism: CapCut's menu is a full-window layered overlay; a click outside the menu content lands on that overlay and
CapCut calls DestroyWindow() on the menu inside its WM_LBUTTONDOWN. The compositor holds an implicit grab on the pressed
surface; once the client destroys that surface, KWin sends the button's release to nobody (focus updates are blocked
while a button is held), and then sends wl_pointer.enter for the main window, which carries no button state.
winewayland therefore never calls NtUserSendHardwareInput for the release: wineserver keeps VK_LBUTTON down, the main
window gets WM_MOUSEMOVE with MK_LBUTTON and no WM_LBUTTONUP, and the application is left with a press that never
ends (Qt keeps its left-button-down state until the next real press).

Windows behavior: there is no implicit grab to lose; the button-up is hardware input that goes to the capture window,
or without one to the window under the cursor (here the main window), when the button is released.

Change: wayland_surface_destroy() records the buttons the driver sent down and not up while the destroyed surface had
the pointer focus. The next pointer event from the compositor (on KWin the enter that follows the physical release; or
a button event) first sends their release with NtUserSendHardwareInput, so wineserver routes WM_xBUTTONUP to the window
under the cursor as on Windows. If a compositor does deliver the real release later, it is dropped, so the application
never gets two. A new press of that button ends the wait. Upstream winewayland has the same gap.

Not covered: if the pointer ends on another client's surface, the release is sent only when it next enters one of
ours (until then GetAsyncKeyState still reports the button down). Pointer-constraint and relative-motion paths are
unchanged.

Lab evidence (desk-b, CapCut 9.7 in a PgI-capcut copy, menu opened and closed by a click on its overlay):
- stock 11.10-181: 0 of 4 click-closes delivered a WM_LBUTTONUP (traces c2t1 x3, c2p1 x1; +waylanddrv shows no
  wl_pointer.button release at all, then pointer enter on the main window ~49 ms after the press).
- 11.10-181e (this patch), traced: 1 of 1 delivered: WM_LBUTTONUP to the main window 47 ms after the press (the
  fakeptr click holds 50 ms); untraced run: the release fired 2 times for 3 click-closes (one not accounted for).
- same build 11.10-181e with NEUTRON_PTR_RELEASE_DESTROYED=0: 0 of 2 click-closes delivered a WM_LBUTTONUP (traced c2v0; WM_MOUSEMOVE with MK_LBUTTON
  only, gate line "= 0 (environment)"); CapCut outcome F,W,F,W (2/4).
- CapCut's own symptom is unchanged by this patch: first-Escape outcome F,W,F,W,F,W (3/6) on 181e on, as on stock
  181 (3/6); with every menu closed by a second Escape instead of a click, the first Escape fails 6/6 and the second
  closes 6/6 on stock 181 -- a separate cause, not this one.
- Photoshop/Premiere menu + Escape check on 11.10-181e: Photoshop 2026 (PgD-ps2 copy): File opened 2/2, Escape
  closed it 2/2. Premiere 2026 (PgD-pr2 copy): cycle 1 opened File and Escape closed the dropdown (bar stays lit in
  menu mode); in cycle 2 the click on File did NOT open it -- the menus team's open bug "after Escape, a click on a
  menu-bar item" (team/menus/menu-bar-escape.mypatch), not this patch: its gate never evaluated in that session (no
  "neutron-gateresolved: NEUTRON_PTR_RELEASE_DESTROYED" among 104 err:waylanddrv lines), so none of its code ran.
  (The copy also showed Premiere's "quit unexpectedly while a project was open" toast at start, carried over from the
  source prefix's earlier session.)

Kill switch: NEUTRON_PTR_RELEASE_DESTROYED=0.


--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
@@ -152,6 +152,9 @@
     BOOL pending_warp;
     uint32_t enter_serial;
     uint32_t button_serial;
+    uint32_t buttons_down;     /* NEUTRON: bit (button - BTN_MOUSE) per button we sent down and not up yet */
+    uint32_t release_pending;  /* NEUTRON: buttons held on a surface we destroyed; the compositor will not send their up */
+    uint32_t released_early;   /* NEUTRON: buttons we released for it; their late real release is dropped */
     struct wayland_cursor cursor;
     double accum_x;
     double accum_y;
@@ -816,6 +819,7 @@
 LRESULT WAYLAND_NotifyIcon(HWND hwnd, UINT msg, NOTIFYICONDATAW *data);
 void WAYLAND_CleanupIcons(HWND hwnd);
 void wayland_keyboard_release_destroyed_focus(void);
+BOOL wayland_pointer_release_destroyed_on(void);
 NTSTATUS waylanddrv_unix_sni_run(void *arg);
 void WAYLAND_SetLayeredWindowAttributes(HWND hwnd, COLORREF key, BYTE alpha, DWORD flags);
 void WAYLAND_SetWindowIcons(HWND hwnd, HICON icon, const ICONINFO *ii, HICON icon_small, const ICONINFO *ii_small);
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_pointer.c
@@ -269,6 +269,71 @@
 
 static void wayland_set_cursor(HWND hwnd, HCURSOR hcursor, BOOL use_hcursor);
 
+/* NEUTRON (2026-11-04): the button release of a press whose window this process destroyed.
+ *
+ * Seen in the lab (CapCut 9.7, 11-03): after Escape closed its menu, the next click on Menu did not reopen it. CapCut closes
+ * its menu when the user clicks outside it: the click lands on the menu's own full-window overlay and CapCut calls
+ * DestroyWindow() on the menu inside its WM_LBUTTONDOWN. The compositor holds an implicit grab on the pressed surface;
+ * once that surface is gone it sends the button's release to nobody, and the next thing we hear is a wl_pointer.enter
+ * on the main window, which carries no button state. Wine never sends the WM_LBUTTONUP, so wineserver keeps VK_LBUTTON
+ * down and the application is left with a press that never ends (measured on KWin, lab 181: 0 of 6 such closes
+ * delivered the release; the main window got WM_MOUSEMOVE with MK_LBUTTON and no WM_LBUTTONUP).
+ *
+ * Windows has no implicit grab to lose: the button-up is a hardware event that goes to the capture window or, without
+ * one, to the window under the cursor, here the main window. So: wayland_surface_destroy() records the buttons still
+ * held on the destroyed pointer-focus surface; the next pointer event from the compositor (the enter that follows the
+ * release on KWin, or a button event) first sends their release, as the hardware would have. If the compositor does
+ * deliver the real release later anyway, it is dropped so the application does not get two. Not covered: a release
+ * while the pointer stays over another client's surface is sent only when the pointer next reaches one of ours.
+ * Kill switch NEUTRON_PTR_RELEASE_DESTROYED=0. */
+BOOL wayland_pointer_release_destroyed_on(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) on = neutron_gate("NEUTRON_PTR_RELEASE_DESTROYED", TRUE);
+    return on;
+}
+
+static uint32_t pointer_button_bit(uint32_t button)
+{
+    return button >= BTN_MOUSE && button < BTN_MOUSE + 32 ? 1u << (button - BTN_MOUSE) : 0;
+}
+
+static void pointer_release_pending(HWND hwnd, const char *where)
+{
+    struct wayland_pointer *pointer = &process_wayland.pointer;
+    uint32_t pending, bit;
+    INPUT input = {0};
+
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&pointer->mutex);
+    pending = pointer->release_pending;
+    pointer->release_pending = 0;
+    pointer->buttons_down &= ~pending;
+    pointer->released_early |= pending;
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&pointer->mutex);
+    if (!pending) return;
+
+    ERR("neutron-ptrrel: buttons %#x were held on a destroyed surface; sending their release at %s (hwnd %p)\n",
+        pending, where, hwnd);
+    input.type = INPUT_MOUSE;
+    for (bit = 0; bit < 32; bit++)
+    {
+        if (!(pending & (1u << bit))) continue;
+        input.mi.mouseData = 0;
+        switch (BTN_MOUSE + bit)
+        {
+        case BTN_LEFT: input.mi.dwFlags = MOUSEEVENTF_LEFTUP; break;
+        case BTN_RIGHT: input.mi.dwFlags = MOUSEEVENTF_RIGHTUP; break;
+        case BTN_MIDDLE: input.mi.dwFlags = MOUSEEVENTF_MIDDLEUP; break;
+        case BTN_SIDE:
+        case BTN_BACK: input.mi.dwFlags = MOUSEEVENTF_XUP; input.mi.mouseData = XBUTTON1; break;
+        case BTN_EXTRA:
+        case BTN_FORWARD: input.mi.dwFlags = MOUSEEVENTF_XUP; input.mi.mouseData = XBUTTON2; break;
+        default: continue;
+        }
+        NtUserSendHardwareInput(hwnd, 0, &input, 0);
+    }
+}
+
 static void pointer_handle_enter(void *data, struct wl_pointer *wl_pointer,
                                  uint32_t serial, struct wl_surface *wl_surface,
                                  wl_fixed_t sx, wl_fixed_t sy)
@@ -290,6 +355,9 @@
     pointer->enter_serial = serial;
     pthread_mutex_unlock(&pointer->mutex);
 
+    /* NEUTRON: on KWin this enter is what follows the release of a press on a surface we destroyed */
+    pointer_release_pending(hwnd, "enter");
+
     /* The cursor is undefined at every enter, so we set it again with
      * the latest information we have. */
     wayland_set_cursor(hwnd, NULL, FALSE);
@@ -322,12 +390,37 @@
                                   uint32_t state)
 {
     struct wayland_pointer *pointer = &process_wayland.pointer;
+    uint32_t bit = pointer_button_bit(button);
     INPUT input = {0};
+    BOOL drop = FALSE;
     HWND hwnd;
 
     InterlockedExchange(&process_wayland.input_serial, serial);
 
-    if (!(hwnd = wayland_pointer_get_focused_hwnd())) return;
+    /* NEUTRON: release what a destroyed surface left held first, then drop the late real release of a button we
+     * already released for it (see pointer_release_pending); a new press of that button ends the wait. */
+    hwnd = wayland_pointer_get_focused_hwnd();
+    pointer_release_pending(hwnd, "button");
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&pointer->mutex);
+    if (state == WL_POINTER_BUTTON_STATE_RELEASED && (pointer->released_early & bit))
+    {
+        pointer->released_early &= ~bit;
+        drop = TRUE;
+    }
+    else if (hwnd && state == WL_POINTER_BUTTON_STATE_PRESSED)
+    {
+        pointer->released_early &= ~bit;
+        pointer->buttons_down |= bit;
+    }
+    else if (hwnd) pointer->buttons_down &= ~bit;
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&pointer->mutex);
+    if (drop)
+    {
+        ERR("neutron-ptrrel: dropping the late release of button %#x (already released)\n", button);
+        return;
+    }
+
+    if (!hwnd) return;
 
     input.type = INPUT_MOUSE;
 
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -444,6 +444,9 @@
     {
         process_wayland.pointer.focused_hwnd = NULL;
         process_wayland.pointer.enter_serial = 0;
+        /* NEUTRON: buttons still held on this surface lose their release (wayland_pointer.c, pointer_release_pending) */
+        if (process_wayland.pointer.buttons_down && wayland_pointer_release_destroyed_on())
+            process_wayland.pointer.release_pending |= process_wayland.pointer.buttons_down;
     }
     if (process_wayland.pointer.constraint_hwnd == surface->hwnd)
         wayland_pointer_clear_constraint();
